%S A402503 3,19,91,211,6931,90091,1531531,9699691,669278611,19409079691,
%T A402503 200560490131,7420738134811,304250263527211,39248283995010091,
%U A402503 614889782588491411,32589158477190044731,5768281050462637917211,351865144078220912949811,7858321551080267055879091,1673822490380096882902246171
%N A402503 a(n) is the number equal to either prime(n)# + 1 (A006862) or 3*prime(n)# + 1 (A402501) that is congruent to 3 modulo 8, where prime(n)# = A002110(n).
%C A402503 For n >= 1, a(n) == 3 (mod 8) is a quadratic residue modulo every prime 3 <= p <= prime(n).
%C A402503 By requiring a(n) == 3 (mod 8), sequence forms a compatible system modulo {A102476(n+1)=4*prime(n)#}: a(n) mod 4*prime(m)# = a(m) for n >= m >= 1.
%C A402503 Let N = A102476(n+1) be the least modulus with 2^(n+1) square roots of 1. The minimal element of the coset of (Z/NZ)*^2 in (Z/NZ)* containing a(n), or the least m coprime to N such that a(n)/m is a square modulo N, is m = A402547(n-1). Here (Z/NZ)* is the multiplicative group of integers modulo N and (Z/NZ)*^2 is the subgroup of squares.
%C A402503 For any fundamental discriminant D of a quadratic field dividing N, kronecker(D,a(n)) = -1 if and only if D == 4 (mod 8) or D == 8 (mod 32).
%H A402503 Jianing Song, <a href="/A402503/b402503.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..350</a>
%F A402503 Let b(n) = A267098(n) = A066490(prime(n)) be the number of primes p <= prime(n) that are congruent to 3 modulo 4. Then a(n) = prime(n)# + 1 if b(n) is even and 3*prime(n)# + 1 if b(n) is odd.
%t A402503 Map[If[Mod[# + 1, 8] == 3, #, 3*#] + 1 &, FoldList[Times, Prime[Range[20]]]] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Nov 22 2026 *)
%o A402503 (PARI) my(P=1, pi43=0); forprime(p=2, 100, P*=p; if(p%4==3, pi43=1-pi43); print1(if(pi43==0, P+1, 3*P+1), ", "); )
%Y A402503 Cf. A002110, A102476, A006862, A402501, A066490, A267098, A402547.
%Y A402503 See A402499 for a list of related sequences.
%K A402503 nonn,easy,new
